// Java Foundations course final MCQ (java-module-check Part 1), graded, 30 questions, 60 minutes. Published in this repository by captain decision (2026-11-10).

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/assessments/java-module-check

::java-final-mcq-01::[html]<p>A program adds up shares of 70 in a loop and then stores a value in a byte.</p><pre><code>public class Main \{
    public static void main(String[] args) \{
        int total \= 0;
        for (int i \= 1; i &lt;\= 4; i++) \{
            total +\= 70 * i / 4;
        \}
        byte b \= (byte) (total + total % 7);
        System.out.println(total + " " + b);
    \}
\}</code></pre><p>What does the program print?</p> {
=174 -76#Correct. Each 70 * i / 4 is integer division, so the terms are 17, 35, 52 and 70 (total 174). 174 % 7 is 6, and casting 180 to byte wraps it past 127 to 180 - 256 \= -76.
~175 -81#Not quite. 175 would need the halves from 70 / 4 and 210 / 4 to be kept, but int division drops them, so the total is 174.
~174 180#Not quite. A byte holds -128 to 127, so the narrowing cast cannot keep 180; it wraps to -76.
~174 127#Not quite. A narrowing cast does not clamp to the largest byte value; it keeps the low 8 bits, so 180 becomes -76.
}

::java-final-mcq-02::[html]<p>The first call prints 2. The second call throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException. You set a breakpoint on the while line and watch i.</p><pre><code>public class Main \{
    static int find(int[] a, int target) \{
        int i \= 0;
        while (a[i] !\= target &amp;&amp; i &lt;\= a.length) \{
            i++;
        \}
        return i;
    \}
    public static void main(String[] args) \{
        System.out.println(find(new int[] \{4, 8, 15\}, 15));
        System.out.println(find(new int[] \{4, 8, 15\}, 16));
    \}
\}</code></pre><p>In the second call, at which value of i does the condition first go wrong, and which loop condition fixes the method?</p> {
~At i \= 3\: change &lt;\= to &lt; and keep the order, while (a[i] !\= target &amp;&amp; i &lt; a.length)#Not quite. a[3] is still read before the bound check runs, so the call still throws at i \= 3.
~At i \= 4\: the loop runs one step too far, so changing &lt;\= to &lt; is enough#Not quite. The exception comes at i \= 3, when a[3] is read; the bound check on the right never gets a chance to stop it.
=At i \= 3\: a[i] is read before the bound is checked. Use while (i &lt; a.length &amp;&amp; a[i] !\= target)#Correct. &amp;&amp; evaluates left to right and skips its right side when the left is false, so the bound check must come first, and it must use &lt; because the last index is a.length - 1.
~At i \= 2\: &amp;&amp; skips the bound check after a match, so use || instead of &amp;&amp;#Not quite. i \= 2 reads 15, which is valid. With || the loop would keep reading past the end even after the bound fails.
}

::java-final-mcq-03::[html]<p>An older method gives points for a level. The intent is 1 for LOW, 10 for MEDIUM and 100 for HIGH. A teammate proposes the switch expression return switch (level) \{ case LOW -&gt; 1; case MEDIUM -&gt; 10; case HIGH -&gt; 100; \};</p><pre><code>public class Main \{
    enum Level \{ LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH \}
    static int points(Level level) \{
        int p \= 0;
        switch (level) \{
            case LOW\:
                p +\= 1;
            case MEDIUM\:
                p +\= 10;
                break;
            case HIGH\:
                p +\= 100;
        \}
        return p;
    \}
    public static void main(String[] args) \{
        System.out.println(points(Level.LOW) + " " + points(Level.HIGH));
    \}
\}</code></pre><p>What does the program print now, and how does the proposed rewrite behave?</p> {
~1 100. Each colon case stops at the next case label; the rewrite behaves the same#Not quite. A case label does not stop execution. Without break, LOW runs on into MEDIUM's statements.
=11 100. LOW falls through into MEDIUM because there is no break; the arrow form never falls through, so it returns 1 for LOW#Correct. In a colon switch, execution continues into the next case until a break. Arrow labels run only their own expression.
~111 100. LOW runs every case below it; the arrow form fixes that#Not quite. The break after MEDIUM's statement ends the switch, so HIGH's statement does not run for LOW.
~11 100. LOW falls through; the arrow form falls through too, so it needs break statements#Not quite. Arrow labels do not fall through, and a switch expression returns the value of the matching case.
}

::java-final-mcq-04::[html]<p>A learner runs mvn -q compile in a small Maven project twice. The first run prints the first message. After one change, the second run prints the second message.</p><pre><code>[ERROR] No compiler is provided in this environment. Perhaps you are running on a JRE rather than a JDK?
&nbsp;
[ERROR] /home/dev/shop/src/main/java/shop/Cart.java\:[14,27] incompatible types\: possible lossy conversion from double to int</code></pre><p>Which pair of fixes matches the two messages, in order?</p> {
~Point JAVA_HOME at a JDK installation; then edit Cart.java at line 27#Not quite. Maven prints the position as [line,column], so the error is on line 14.
~Reinstall Maven, because Maven brings its own compiler; then edit Cart.java at line 14#Not quite. Maven uses the compiler from the JDK it runs on, so reinstalling Maven does not help while JAVA_HOME names a runtime without javac.
~Put the JRE's bin directory first on the PATH; then rerun the build, because the second message clears on retry#Not quite. A JRE has no javac, and a compile error stays until the source line is fixed.
=Point JAVA_HOME at a JDK installation, which includes javac; then edit Cart.java at line 14 so the double value is not stored in an int without a cast#Correct. A JRE can run programs but has no compiler, so Maven needs JAVA_HOME to name a JDK. In [14,27], 14 is the line and 27 is the column.
}

::java-final-mcq-05::[html]<p>A program cleans up an order code.</p><pre><code>public class Main \{
    public static void main(String[] args) \{
        String raw \= "  order-42  ";
        raw.strip();
        String s \= raw.strip();
        int dash \= s.indexOf('-');
        s.toUpperCase();
        String id \= s.substring(dash + 1);
        System.out.println("[" + s.substring(0, dash) + "|" + id + "|" + raw.length() + "]");
    \}
\}</code></pre><p>What does the program print?</p> {
~[ORDER|42|12]#Not quite. s.toUpperCase() returns a new String, and the result is thrown away, so s stays lower case.
=[order|42|12]#Correct. strip and toUpperCase return new strings; the calls whose results are not stored change nothing. s is "order-42", the dash is at index 5, and raw still has its 12 characters.
~[order|42|8]#Not quite. raw.strip() returns a new String; raw itself still holds the 4 spaces, so its length is 12.
~[order-|42|12]#Not quite. substring(0, dash) stops before index dash, so the dash is not included.
}

::java-final-mcq-06::[html]<p>Two overloaded static methods share one static counter.</p><pre><code>public class Main \{
    static int calls \= 0;
    static String size(int n) \{ calls++; return "int"; \}
    static String size(double d) \{ calls +\= 10; return "double"; \}
    public static void main(String[] args) \{
        long big \= 5L;
        String a \= size(3);
        String b \= size(3.0);
        String c \= size(big);
        System.out.println(a + " " + b + " " + c + " " + calls);
    \}
\}</code></pre><p>What does the program print?</p> {
~int double int 12#Not quite. Converting long to int is a narrowing conversion, which the compiler never applies on its own when it picks an overload.
~A compile error, because no overload takes a long#Not quite. A widening conversion from long to double lets size(double) accept the call.
~int double double 3#Not quite. calls is a static field, and size(double) adds 10 to it each time.
=int double double 21#Correct. There is no size(long), and long cannot narrow to int without a cast, so the compiler widens big to double. calls is one static field shared by all calls\: 1 + 10 + 10.
}

::java-final-mcq-07::[html]<p>The rule is\: 85 to 100 is merit, 50 to 84 is pass, 0 to 49 is fail, and anything else is invalid. A tester tries grade(60), grade(49), grade(85) and grade(-1).</p><pre><code>static String grade(int score) \{
    if (score &lt; 0 || score &gt; 100) return "invalid";
    else if (score &gt;\= 50) return "pass";
    else if (score &gt;\= 85) return "merit";
    else return "fail";
\}</code></pre><p>Which call exposes the defect, and what fixes it?</p> {
=grade(85), which returns pass; test score &gt;\= 85 before score &gt;\= 50#Correct. Every score of 85 or more also satisfies score &gt;\= 50, so the merit branch is unreachable. Checking the narrower condition first fixes it. The boundary value 85 is the test that reveals this.
~grade(49), which returns pass; change score &gt;\= 50 to score &gt; 50#Not quite. 49 fails score &gt;\= 50 and returns fail, as the rule requires.
~grade(-1), which returns fail; move the invalid check to the end#Not quite. -1 is caught by the first condition and returns invalid. Moving that check last would break it.
~grade(60), which returns merit; swap the two return values#Not quite. 60 returns pass, as the rule requires.
}

::java-final-mcq-08::[html]<p>A Box keeps a final reference to an array. The same array is also passed as a second argument.</p><pre><code>public class Main \{
    static void fill(Box box, int[] other) \{
        box.cells[0] \= 7;
        other \= new int[] \{9, 9\};
        other[1] \= 5;
    \}
    public static void main(String[] args) \{
        int[] data \= \{1, 2\};
        Box box \= new Box(data);
        int[] spare \= data;
        fill(box, spare);
        System.out.println(data[0] + " " + data[1] + " " + spare[0]);
    \}
\}
class Box \{
    final int[] cells;
    Box(int[] cells) \{ this.cells \= cells; \}
\}</code></pre><p>What does the program print?</p> {
~1 2 1#Not quite. final on cells stops the field from being pointed at another array; the elements of that array can still change.
~7 5 7#Not quite. Java copies the reference into other. After other \= new int[] ..., other refers to a new array, and writing to it does not touch data.
=7 2 7#Correct. data, spare and box.cells all refer to one array, so writing through the final field changes it; final stops reassigning cells, not changing elements. Reassigning the parameter other only changes the method's copy of the reference.
~7 2 9#Not quite. spare still refers to the original array; the method cannot change which array the caller's variable refers to.
}

::java-final-mcq-09::[html]<p>mirror is meant to leave out holding the reverse of "abcde", which is "edcba". It reads one character from each end and recurses on the part in between.</p><pre><code>public class Main \{
    static void mirror(String s, int i, int j, StringBuilder out) \{
        if (i &gt;\= j) \{
            if (i \=\= j) out.append(s.charAt(i));
            return;
        \}
        out.append(s.charAt(j));
        mirror(s, i, j, out);
        out.append(s.charAt(i));
    \}
    public static void main(String[] args) \{
        StringBuilder out \= new StringBuilder();
        mirror("abcde", 0, 4, out);
        System.out.println(out);
    \}
\}</code></pre><p>What happens when the program runs, and which recursive call fixes it?</p> {
~It ends in StringIndexOutOfBoundsException, because j keeps decreasing; use mirror(s, i + 1, j, out)#Not quite. Neither index changes, so no index leaves the string; the calls pile up until the stack overflows. Moving only i also gives the wrong text.
~It prints e, because the base case runs on the second call; use mirror(s, i + 1, j - 1, out)#Not quite. The second call gets the same 0 and 4, so the base case is never reached.
=It ends in StackOverflowError, because the call passes the same i and j, so i &gt;\= j never becomes true; use mirror(s, i + 1, j - 1, out)#Correct. Each call must move both indexes toward each other so they meet (odd length) or cross (even length) and the base case runs. Every charAt index stays inside the string.
~It ends in StackOverflowError; use mirror(s, i + 1, j + 1, out)#Not quite. Moving both indexes up keeps them the same distance apart, so they never meet, and j soon passes the end of the string.
}

::java-final-mcq-10::[html]<p>A recursive method finds the largest value in an array, starting at index i.</p><pre><code>public class Main \{
    static int best(int[] a, int i) \{
        if (i \=\= a.length - 1) \{
            return a[i];
        \}
        int rest \= best(a, i + 1);
        if (a[i] &gt; rest) \{
            return a[i];
        \}
        return rest;
    \}
    public static void main(String[] args) \{
        int[] a \= new int[4];
        a[1] \= 6;
        a[3] \= a[1] - 8;
        System.out.println(best(a, 0));
    \}
\}</code></pre><p>What does the program print, and how many frames of best are on the call stack while the deepest call runs?</p> {
=6, with 4 frames of best on the stack#Correct. new int[4] starts as 0, 0, 0, 0; then a is 0, 6, 0, -2. best(a, 0) calls best(a, 1), best(a, 2) and best(a, 3), which returns -2 at the base case; the returns compare upward and keep 6.
~6, with 1 frame of best on the stack#Not quite. Each call waits for best(a, i + 1) to return, so its frame stays on the stack\: 4 frames when best(a, 3) runs.
~-2, with 4 frames of best on the stack#Not quite. -2 is only the base case value. Each returning frame compares it with its own element and keeps the larger one.
~0, with 3 frames of best on the stack#Not quite. The unset elements are 0, but a[1] is 6. The calls run for i \= 0, 1, 2 and 3, which is 4 frames.
}

::java-final-mcq-11::[html]<p>An AI assistant suggests this code and says\: "It prints 2, the index of 19." You check the claim by compiling the code and reading the Collections.binarySearch documentation.</p><pre><code>List&lt;int&gt; nums \= new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of(42, 7, 19, 3));
int pos \= Collections.binarySearch(nums, 19);
System.out.println(pos);</code></pre><p>What do you find?</p> {
~It compiles and prints 2, because binarySearch checks every element when the list is unsorted#Not quite. List&lt;int&gt; does not compile, and binary search never falls back to checking every element.
~It compiles because of autoboxing, and binarySearch sorts the list first, so it prints the index of 19 in sorted order#Not quite. Autoboxing converts values, not type arguments, so List&lt;int&gt; is still a compile error; binarySearch does not sort.
~It does not compile, because List.of cannot be passed to an ArrayList constructor; with that fixed it prints 2#Not quite. new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(List.of(...)) is valid; the error is the primitive type argument, and the unsorted input still makes the result undefined.
=It does not compile, because a type argument cannot be a primitive type, so it needs List&lt;Integer&gt;; even then the list is unsorted, and the documentation says the result is undefined, so 2 is not guaranteed#Correct. Generics take reference types, so int must be the wrapper Integer. Binary search halves a sorted range; on unsorted input its result is undefined.
}

::java-final-mcq-12::[html]<p>A loop is meant to reverse the array in place with two indexes.</p><pre><code>public class Main \{
    public static void main(String[] args) \{
        int[] a \= \{1, 2, 3, 4\};
        for (int i \= 0, j \= a.length; i &lt; j; i++, j--) \{
            int t \= a[i];
            a[i] \= a[j];
            a[j] \= t;
        \}
        for (int x \: a) \{
            System.out.print(x + " ");
        \}
    \}
\}</code></pre><p>What happens, and what fixes it?</p> {
~It prints 4 3 2 1#Not quite. The first swap reads a[4], which does not exist.
=It throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException on the first swap; start j at a.length - 1#Correct. The last valid index of a 4-element array is 3, so a[j] with j \= 4 is out of bounds before any swap finishes. With j \= 3 the loop swaps (0, 3) and (1, 2), then stops when the indexes cross.
~It prints 1 2 3 4, because the swaps undo each other; stop at i &lt; j / 2#Not quite. The loop already stops when i reaches j, and the first swap fails before anything is printed.
~It throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException on the last swap; change the condition to i &lt;\= j#Not quite. The first swap already uses index 4, and i &lt;\= j would also swap the middle element with itself.
}

::java-final-mcq-13::[html]<p>A list of words is edited and then counted into a map.</p><pre><code>import java.util.*;
public class Main \{
    public static void main(String[] args) \{
        List&lt;String&gt; words \= new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
        words.add("tea");
        words.add("cake");
        words.add("tea");
        words.add("jam");
        words.set(1, "tea");
        words.remove(3);
        Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; counts \= new HashMap&lt;&gt;();
        for (String w \: words) \{
            counts.put(w, counts.getOrDefault(w, 0) + 1);
        \}
        System.out.println(counts.get("tea") + " " + counts.containsKey("jam") + " " + counts.size());
    \}
\}</code></pre><p>What does the program print?</p> {
~2 true 3#Not quite. That ignores the edits. set replaces the element at index 1, and remove(3) removes jam before counting.
=3 false 1#Correct. set(1, ...) replaces cake, so the list is tea, tea, tea, jam; remove(3) removes the element at index 3, jam. The map then has one key, tea, with count 3.
~4 false 1#Not quite. set replaces the element at an index; it does not insert a new one, so there are three teas.
~3 false 3#Not quite. size counts keys, not put calls. put on an existing key replaces its value.
}

::java-final-mcq-14::[html]<p>A stock program stops with this error. Ink that is not in the map should count as 0 in stock.</p><pre><code>import java.util.*;
public class Main \{
    public static void main(String[] args) \{
        Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; stock \= new HashMap&lt;&gt;();
        stock.put("pen", 12);
        int left \= stock.get("ink") - 1;
        System.out.println(left);
    \}
\}
&nbsp;
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.NullPointerException\: Cannot invoke "java.lang.Integer.intValue()" because the return value of "java.util.Map.get(Object)" is null
    at Main.main(Main.java\:6)</code></pre><p>What was null, and which change keeps the program running as intended?</p> {
~The map stock; create it with new HashMap&lt;&gt;() before calling get#Not quite. stock was created on the line before; the message names the return value of get, not the map.
~The variable left; declare it as Integer instead of int so it can hold null#Not quite. stock.get("ink") - 1 still unboxes the null to subtract, so the same exception is thrown before anything is stored.
=The Integer that get returned for the missing key "ink"; use stock.getOrDefault("ink", 0) - 1#Correct. The message says the return value of Map.get was null, and unboxing that null to subtract 1 failed. getOrDefault supplies 0 for a missing key, so the program prints -1.
~The Integer that get returned; cast it with (int) stock.get("ink") - 1#Not quite. Casting a null Integer to int unboxes it too, so the program still throws NullPointerException; the missing key needs a default value.
}

::java-final-mcq-15::[html]<p>Two classes live in different packages.</p><pre><code>// shop/Item.java
package shop;
public class Item \{
    String name;
    private int qty;
    public Item(String name, int qty) \{ this.name \= name; this.qty \= qty; \}
    public int qty() \{ return qty; \}
    void restock(int n) \{ qty +\= n; \}
\}
&nbsp;
// app/Main.java
package app;
import shop.Item;
public class Main \{
    public static void main(String[] args) \{
        Item a \= new Item("pen", 3);   // line A
        Item b \= new Item();           // line B
        a.restock(2);                  // line C
        System.out.println(a.qty());   // line D
    \}
\}</code></pre><p>Which marked lines of app/Main.java fail to compile?</p> {
~Only line C#Not quite. Once a class declares any constructor, the compiler no longer supplies a default one, so new Item() fails too.
~Only line B#Not quite. restock has no access modifier, so only code in package shop can call it.
~Lines B, C and D, because qty is private#Not quite. The field qty is private, but line D calls the public method qty(), which is allowed.
=Lines B and C#Correct. Item declares a constructor, so the compiler supplies no no-argument one (line B). restock has no modifier, so it is package-private and invisible from package app (line C). The public constructor and public qty() work.
}

::java-final-mcq-16::[html]<p>Two accounts are created and updated.</p><pre><code>public class Main \{
    public static void main(String[] args) \{
        Account x \= new Account(50);
        Account y \= new Account(80);
        y.setBalance(-5);
        x.setBalance(30);
        System.out.println(x.getBalance() + " " + y.getBalance());
    \}
\}
class Account \{
    private int balance;
    Account(int balance) \{
        balance \= balance;
    \}
    void setBalance(int value) \{
        if (value &gt;\= 0) \{
            this.balance \= value;
        \}
    \}
    int getBalance() \{ return balance; \}
\}</code></pre><p>What does the program print?</p> {
=30 0#Correct. In the constructor, balance \= balance assigns the parameter to itself, so the field keeps its default 0; this.balance \= balance would fix it. The setter rejects -5, so y stays 0, and each object has its own field.
~30 80#Not quite. Inside the constructor the name balance means the parameter, so the field is never set and stays 0.
~30 -5#Not quite. setBalance only stores values that are 0 or more, so -5 is rejected.
~30 30#Not quite. x and y are separate objects with separate balance fields; setting x does not change y.
}

::java-final-mcq-17::[html]<p>A class and a record hold the same kind of data.</p><pre><code>public class Main \{
    public static void main(String[] args) \{
        Tag tag \= new Tag("red");
        Label label \= new Label("red");
        System.out.println(tag.equals(new Tag("red")) + " "
            + label.equals(new Label("red")) + " "
            + (label \=\= new Label("red")));
    \}
\}
class Tag \{
    final String name;
    Tag(String name) \{ this.name \= name; \}
\}
record Label(String name) \{\}</code></pre><p>What does the program print?</p> {
~true true false#Not quite. Without an equals override, Tag uses Object's equals, which is the same as \=\=, so two separate Tag objects are not equal.
~false false false#Not quite. A record's generated equals compares its components, so two Labels holding "red" are equal.
~false true true#Not quite. \=\= compares references, even for records; the record's generated equals is only used when equals is called.
=false true false#Correct. Tag does not override equals, so it inherits Object's equals, which compares references, and two separate Tag objects are not equal. A record's generated equals compares components. \=\= always compares references.
}

::java-final-mcq-18::[html]<p>A three-level class hierarchy prints from each constructor. The commented line in main is line W.</p><pre><code>public class Main \{
    public static void main(String[] args) \{
        Vehicle v \= new SportsCar();
        System.out.println(" " + v.sound());
        // Vehicle w \= new Vehicle();   // line W
    \}
\}
abstract class Vehicle \{
    Vehicle() \{ System.out.print("V"); \}
    abstract String sound();
\}
class Car extends Vehicle \{
    Car() \{ super(); System.out.print("C"); \}
    String sound() \{ return "beep"; \}
\}
class SportsCar extends Car \{
    SportsCar() \{ System.out.print("S"); \}
    String sound() \{ return "vroom"; \}
\}</code></pre><p>What does the program print, and what happens if line W is uncommented?</p> {
~SCV vroom; line W compiles because Vehicle has a constructor#Not quite. The superclass constructor finishes before the subclass constructor body runs, and a constructor does not make an abstract class instantiable.
=VCS vroom; line W fails to compile because Vehicle is abstract#Correct. Every constructor first calls its superclass constructor (SportsCar's call to super() is implicit), so V prints first. sound() runs the version of the runtime type, SportsCar. An abstract class cannot be instantiated.
~VS vroom; line W fails to compile#Not quite. SportsCar's constructor implicitly calls Car(), which prints C, so all three letters print.
~VCS beep; line W fails to compile#Not quite. SportsCar overrides sound(), and the overriding version runs even through a Vehicle reference.
}

::java-final-mcq-19::[html]<p>A Truck reuses Engine's methods by extending it. A reviewer says Truck should not extend Engine.</p><pre><code>class Engine \{
    void start() \{ System.out.println("start"); \}
    int rpm() \{ return 900; \}
\}
class Truck extends Engine \{
    void drive() \{ start(); System.out.println("rpm " + rpm()); \}
\}</code></pre><p>Which reasoning and rewrite fit?</p> {
~A truck is an engine (is-a), so extends is right; mark Engine final to stop other subclasses#Not quite. A truck is not a kind of engine, and making Engine final would stop Truck from extending it at all.
~Make Engine abstract and keep extends, because an abstract parent fixes the relationship#Not quite. Making a class abstract does not change whether the relationship is is-a or has-a.
=A truck has an engine (has-a), so Truck should hold a private final Engine field set in its constructor and call engine.start() and engine.rpm() in drive()#Correct. Inheritance fits an is-a relationship. Here Truck only needs Engine's behavior, so delegating to an Engine field gives the same output without making every Truck an Engine.
~Has-a, so Engine should extend Truck instead, so the engine can call drive()#Not quite. Reversing the inheritance still uses is-a, and an engine is not a kind of truck.
}

::java-final-mcq-20::[html]<p>One class overrides an interface's default method; an anonymous class does not. A Predicate decides what to print.</p><pre><code>import java.util.*;
import java.util.function.Predicate;
public class Main \{
    public static void main(String[] args) \{
        Greeter anon \= new Greeter() \{
            public String name() \{ return "Ana"; \}
        \};
        Predicate&lt;Greeter&gt; polite \= g -&gt; g.greet().charAt(0) \=\= 'H';
        for (Greeter g \: List.of(new Robot(), anon)) \{
            if (polite.test(g)) System.out.println(g.greet());
            else System.out.println(g.name());
        \}
    \}
\}
interface Greeter \{
    String name();
    default String greet() \{ return "Hi " + name(); \}
\}
class Robot implements Greeter \{
    public String name() \{ return "R2D2"; \}
    public String greet() \{ return "Beep " + name(); \}
\}</code></pre><p>What does the program print?</p> {
=R2D2 and then Hi Ana, on two lines#Correct. Robot's own greet replaces the default, so its greeting starts with B and the predicate is false\: the name prints. The anonymous class only implements name(), so it keeps the default greet, which starts with H.
~Hi R2D2 and then Hi Ana#Not quite. A class's own method replaces the interface default, so Robot greets with Beep, and the predicate is false for it.
~Beep R2D2 and then Ana#Not quite. That inverts the predicate\: test returns true only when the greeting starts with H.
~A compile error, because the anonymous class must also implement greet()#Not quite. A default method has a body, so an implementing class may keep it; only the abstract method name() must be implemented.
}

::java-final-mcq-21::[html]<p>Two versions of a service count stored entries. Store is an interface. Stores.create() is a static factory that returns the production Store. A test needs count() to use an in-memory Store with 3 entries.</p><pre><code>class ReportService \{
    private final Store store \= Stores.create();
    int count() \{ return store.size(); \}
\}
&nbsp;
class ReportService2 \{
    private final Store store;
    ReportService2(Store store) \{ this.store \= store; \}
    int count() \{ return store.size(); \}
\}</code></pre><p>Which statement is right?</p> {
=Only ReportService2 lets the test pass in its own Store; ReportService always gets whatever Stores.create() returns, and the factory hides which class that is#Correct. Taking the interface through the constructor (dependency inversion) lets a test supply a different implementation. A static factory hides the concrete class from the caller, but here the service calls it itself, so the test cannot choose.
~Only ReportService, because a static factory can return any implementation, so the test can choose one#Not quite. The factory chooses the implementation, not the test; ReportService gives the test no way to pass one in.
~Both, because Store is an interface#Not quite. Depending on an interface helps only when the implementation can be supplied from outside, as the constructor of ReportService2 allows.
~Neither\: a test must use the production Store, because dependency inversion only matters in production code#Not quite. Dependency inversion is what lets a test supply its own implementation.
}

::java-final-mcq-22::[html]<p>LoadException is a checked exception with a constructor LoadException(String message, Throwable cause). The method below has two gaps\: HEADER after the parameter list and THROW in the catch block.</p><pre><code>static String load(Path p) HEADER \{
    try \{
        return Files.readString(p);
    \} catch (IOException e) \{
        THROW
    \}
\}</code></pre><p>Which pair compiles and keeps the original IOException as the cause?</p> {
~HEADER\: nothing; THROW\: throw new LoadException("cannot load " + p, e);#Not quite. LoadException is checked, so the compiler reports it as unreported unless load declares it with throws.
~HEADER\: throws LoadException; THROW\: throw new LoadException(e.getMessage(), null);#Not quite. This compiles, but passing null as the cause loses the original IOException and its stack trace.
~HEADER\: throws IOException; THROW\: throw new LoadException("cannot load " + p, e);#Not quite. LoadException extends Exception, not IOException, so declaring IOException does not cover it, and the code does not compile.
=HEADER\: throws LoadException; THROW\: throw new LoadException("cannot load " + p, e);#Correct. A checked exception must be declared with throws (or handled), and passing e as the cause keeps the low-level failure in the Caused by\: chain.
}

::java-final-mcq-23::[html]<p>An import fails. Before the stack trace, the program prints two lines, close and finally, in some order. The stack trace is\:</p><pre><code>import java.io.IOException;
public class Main \{
    static class Conn implements AutoCloseable \{
        public void close() \{ System.out.println("close"); \}
    \}
    static String fetch(int id) throws IOException \{
        throw new IOException("row " + id + " unreadable");
    \}
    static String load(int id) throws ImportException \{
        try (Conn c \= new Conn()) \{
            return fetch(id);
        \} catch (IOException e) \{
            throw new ImportException("import failed", e);
        \} finally \{
            System.out.println("finally");
        \}
    \}
    public static void main(String[] args) throws ImportException \{
        load(7);
    \}
\}
class ImportException extends Exception \{
    ImportException(String message, Throwable cause) \{ super(message, cause); \}
\}
&nbsp;
Exception in thread "main" ImportException\: import failed
    at Main.load(Main.java\:13)
    at Main.main(Main.java\:19)
Caused by\: java.io.IOException\: row 7 unreadable
    at Main.fetch(Main.java\:7)
    at Main.load(Main.java\:11)
    ... 1 more</code></pre><p>Which statement is right?</p> {
~The root cause is the ImportException thrown in load; finally prints before close#Not quite. ImportException wraps the real failure; the Caused by\: line names the IOException. The resource closes before finally runs.
~The root cause is the IOException thrown in fetch; finally prints before close, because the resource is closed when load returns#Not quite. try-with-resources closes the resource when the try block ends, before catch and finally.
=The root cause is the IOException thrown in fetch; close prints before finally, because the resource is closed as the try block ends, before the catch and finally blocks run#Correct. Follow Caused by\: to the last exception in the chain and its first own-code line. try-with-resources closes the resource before catch runs, and finally runs last, even though catch throws.
~The root cause is the IOException thrown in fetch; close never prints, because the try block ended with an exception#Not quite. try-with-resources closes the resource however the try block ends, normally or with an exception.
}

::java-final-mcq-24::[html]<p>A parser catches two kinds of unchecked exception. NumberFormatException is a subclass of RuntimeException.</p><pre><code>public class Main \{
    static int parse(String s) \{
        try \{
            return Integer.parseInt(s.strip());
        \} catch (NumberFormatException e) \{
            return -1;
        \} catch (RuntimeException e) \{
            return -2;
        \}
    \}
    public static void main(String[] args) \{
        System.out.println(parse(" 12 ") + " " + parse("x") + " " + parse(null));
    \}
\}</code></pre><p>What does the program print, and what happens if the two catch blocks swap places?</p> {
~12 -1 -1; after the swap the code compiles and behaves the same#Not quite. The null input throws NullPointerException, which only the RuntimeException catch handles. A broader catch before a narrower one does not compile.
=12 -1 -2; after the swap the code does not compile, because RuntimeException would already catch every NumberFormatException#Correct. "x" fails parsing, which the specific catch handles; null.strip() throws NullPointerException, another RuntimeException. Catch blocks go from most to least specific, or the later one is unreachable.
~12 -2 -2; after the swap the code does not compile#Not quite. The first matching catch runs, and NumberFormatException matches the first catch exactly, so "x" gives -1.
~12 -1, then the program stops with NullPointerException; after the swap the code does not compile#Not quite. NullPointerException is a RuntimeException, so the second catch handles it and returns -2.
}

::java-final-mcq-25::[html]<p>A Javalin book API registers exception handlers that map BadInputException to 400 and NotFoundException to 404, each with a JSON error body such as \{"error"\: "book 99 not found"\}, and the app's own catch-all handler for any other exception answers 500 and is the one place the app logs that failure. Three requests arrive\: (1) POST /books with a blank title; (2) GET /books/99 for a book that does not exist; (3) GET /books/5, where the book store fails with an unexpected IllegalStateException.</p><p>Which statuses fit requests 1 to 3, and at which level should the catch-all handler log the failure in request 3?</p> {
~404, 400, 500; log request 3 at ERROR with its stack trace#Not quite. Invalid input is 400 Bad Request, and a resource that does not exist is 404 Not Found.
~400, 404, 500; log request 3 at INFO, because the client still received a response#Not quite. INFO is for normal events. Request 3 is an operation that failed, which is what ERROR is for.
~400, 500, 500; log request 3 at WARN, because the handler caught it#Not quite. A missing book is a client-side 404, not a server failure, and an operation that failed is logged at ERROR, not WARN.
=400, 404, 500; log request 3 at ERROR with its stack trace#Correct. A blank title is invalid input from the client (400), a missing book is 404, and an unexpected storage failure is a server error (500). ERROR is for an operation that failed, and logging the exception keeps its stack trace.
}

::java-final-mcq-26::[html]<p>A book API registers the handler below, backed by the in-memory book store shown. A client sends POST /books with the body \{"title"\: "Dune"\}, which carries no id. The server receives it and its save completes, but the response is lost when the network drops, so the client times out and sends the same request again, which also reaches the server.</p><pre><code>config.routes.post("/books", ctx -&gt; \{
    Book book \= ctx.bodyAsClass(Book.class);
    Book saved \= books.save(book);
    ctx.status(201).json(saved);
\});
&nbsp;
// in class BookStore\: no duplicate check
public Book save(Book book) \{
    Book saved \= new Book(nextId++, book.title());
    store.put(saved.id(), saved);
    return saved;
\}</code></pre><p>What does the server end up with, and what does each request get back?</p> {
~One stored book; the second request gets 201 with the same JSON, because Javalin ignores repeated requests#Not quite. Javalin runs the handler for every request it receives, so the second POST saves again.
=Two stored books with different ids; the server answers each request with 201 and that request's saved book as JSON, and the client sees only the second answer. POST is not idempotent; a PUT to the book's own URL with the full book would leave one#Correct. Each POST runs the handler again, and this store gives each save a new id, so a second book is stored. Repeating an idempotent request, such as PUT to /books/\{id\}, leaves the same state as sending it once.
~One stored book, because POST is idempotent; the second request gets 200#Not quite. POST is not idempotent, and this handler always sets 201.
~Two stored books; the second request gets 409, because the handler detects the duplicate#Not quite. Nothing in the handler checks for duplicates, and it always sets 201.
}

::java-final-mcq-27::[html]<p>A Javalin app receives this request URL\: http\://localhost\:7070/books/42?format\=short. Its handler is a method reference to a method of a controller object, books, which reads from a book store.</p><pre><code>config.routes.get("/books/\{id\}", books\:\:show);
&nbsp;
// in class BookController
void show(Context ctx) \{
    String id \= ctx.pathParam("id");
    String format \= ctx.queryParam("format");
    ctx.json(store.find(id, format));
\}</code></pre><p>Which reading of the URL and the handler is right?</p> {
~Host localhost\:7070, path /books, query 42?format\=short; pathParam("id") returns null#Not quite. The port is its own part after the colon, and the path is /books/42; the query starts after the ?.
~Scheme http, host localhost, port 7070, path /books/42?format\=short; queryParam("format") returns null because the query is part of the path#Not quite. The ? ends the path and starts the query, and queryParam reads it.
=Scheme http, host localhost, port 7070, path /books/42, query format\=short; pathParam("id") returns "42" and queryParam("format") returns "short"#Correct. The route pattern /books/\{id\} matches the path, so the \{id\} part is a path parameter; the text after ? is the query, read with queryParam. books\:\:show passes each request's Context to show.
~Scheme http, host localhost, port 7070, path /books/\{id\}, query format\=short; queryParam("id") returns "42"#Not quite. \{id\} appears in the route pattern, not in the URL, and 42 is a path parameter read with pathParam.
}

::java-final-mcq-28::[html]<p>A handler is written as an anonymous class. Javalin's Handler interface has one abstract method, void handle(Context ctx). A second interface, Pair, declares two abstract methods, void first() and void second().</p><pre><code>Handler h \= new Handler() \{
    @Override
    public void handle(Context ctx) \{
        ctx.result("pong");
    \}
\};</code></pre><p>Which lambda is equivalent, and can Pair be the target of a lambda?</p> {
=Handler h \= ctx -&gt; ctx.result("pong"); Pair cannot be a lambda target, because a lambda implements exactly one abstract method#Correct. The lambda's single parameter and body become the one abstract method handle. An interface with two abstract methods is not a functional interface.
~Handler h \= () -&gt; ctx.result("pong"); Pair cannot be a lambda target#Not quite. handle takes one parameter, so the lambda must declare one; with () there is no ctx to use.
~Handler h \= ctx -&gt; ctx.result("pong"); Pair can be a lambda target, because both of its methods return void#Not quite. A lambda target needs exactly one abstract method; return types do not change that.
~Handler h \= ctx -&gt; \{ return "pong"; \}; Pair cannot be a lambda target#Not quite. handle returns void, so the lambda cannot return a value; the response is set with ctx.result.
}

::java-final-mcq-29::[html]<p>Checkout saves an order only when its total is above 0. A team writes two tests. Goal 1\: after two valid orders are placed, the repository holds both. Goal 2\: an order with total 0 never causes a save call.</p><pre><code>interface OrderRepo \{
    void save(String orderId);
    List&lt;String&gt; findAll();
\}
&nbsp;
class Checkout \{
    private final OrderRepo repo;
    Checkout(OrderRepo repo) \{ this.repo \= repo; \}
    void place(String orderId, int total) \{
        if (total &gt; 0) repo.save(orderId);
    \}
\}</code></pre><p>Which test doubles suit the two goals?</p> {
~Goal 1\: a stub whose findAll always returns a list of two order ids; Goal 2\: a Mockito mock with verify(repo, never()).save(anyString())#Not quite. A stub returns the canned list whatever Checkout did, so the test would pass even if nothing was saved.
~Goal 1\: a hand-written in-memory fake; Goal 2\: a stub whose save does nothing#Not quite. A stub that does nothing records nothing, so the test cannot tell whether save was called.
~Goal 1\: a Mockito mock with verify(repo).save(anyString()); Goal 2\: a stub whose findAll returns an empty list#Not quite. verify(repo) with no count checks for exactly one call, not two stored orders, and an empty canned list says nothing about whether save was called.
=Goal 1\: a hand-written in-memory fake whose findAll returns what save stored; Goal 2\: a Mockito mock with verify(repo, never()).save(anyString())#Correct. A fake keeps real state, so the test reads back what Checkout saved. A mock records calls, so verify with never() can prove a call did not happen.
}

::java-final-mcq-30::[html]<p>The rule\: totals of 100 or more get 10 off; smaller totals pay in full. The only test, assertEquals(140, pay(150)), passes. JaCoCo reports 100% line coverage and 1 of 2 branches missed for pay.</p><pre><code>static int pay(int total) \{
    int off \= 10;
    if (total &gt;\= 100) \{
        off \= 10;
    \}
    return total - off;
\}</code></pre><p>What does the report show, and which test should be added next?</p> {
~100% line coverage shows the method is right, so no test is needed#Not quite. Coverage measures what ran, not whether it gave the right result, and one branch has never run.
~Add assertEquals(40, pay(50)), which covers the missed branch and passes#Not quite. It covers the branch, but 40 is what the code returns now, not what the rule requires, so the test locks the defect in.
=Every line ran, but the false branch of the if never did; add assertEquals(50, pay(50)), which fails and exposes the defect#Correct. Line coverage only says each line ran at least once; it cannot say results are right. A total under 100 takes the missed branch, and the expected value 50 comes from the rule, so the test fails\: off stays 10.
~Add assertEquals(90, pay(100)) to cover the missed branch#Not quite. 100 takes the true branch again, so the false branch is still untested.
}
